{"type": "sena", "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "lesson_name", "content": ""}, {"type": "unit_name", "content": "Exception Handling in C#"}, {"type": "position", "content": 11}, {"type": "origin", "content": "stub"}, {"type": "lesson", "content": 3}, {"type": "level", "content": "B1.1"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"type": "test", "content": "false"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content": "These are words you hear to talk about the concept of exception handling in C#."}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"type": "primary", "content": "Talk about principles, concepts, characteristics of general-purpose computer programming language in spoken and written situations."}, {"type": "secondary_1", "content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level.  Exchanges simple information on principles, concepts, and features of general purpose computer programming languages."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"type": "background_image", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob11re1img01.jpg"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Will Be Able to Talk About Future Ability or Possibility"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use <b>be able to</b> for general ability and possibility. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "You use <b>will be able to</b> to talk about possibility or ability in the future. <b>Will be able to</b> works as the future form of the verb <b>can</b> due to the impossibility of mixing the two verbs <b>will</b> and <b>can</b> in a single sentence.  </br></br> Example:</br></br>  <li><i>You will be able to handle exceptions in C#</i>."}, {"type": "text", "content": "<strong>Structure:</strong></br> "}, {"type": "table", "content": "{\"rows\":3,\"columns\":4,\"cells\":[\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong>Subject</strong>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong>will</strong>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong>be able to</strong>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<strong>infinitive</strong>\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<i>Ruth\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<i>will\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<i>be able to \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<i>explain the concept.\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<i>Baxter\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<i>will\\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<i>be able to \\\"}\",\"{\\\"type\\\": \\\"text\\\", \\\"content\\\": \\\"<i>help.\\\"}\"],\"styles\":\"\"}"}, {"type": "example", "content": "At the end of this session, you will be able to manage a few keywords necessary to implement exception handling in your code."}, {"type": "example", "content": "With this keyword, you will be able to set the reaction of the program or application each time an error appears."}, {"type": "example", "content": "With this keyword, you will be able to send a new action through the code to verify no error from a chain of operations is left with no solution."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Quantifiers, a Few"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use quantifiers when you want to express the amount of something."}, {"type": "text", "content": "You use <strong>few</strong> or <strong>a few</strong> with plural countable nouns.</br></br>   When you use <strong>a few</strong>, it means a small amount but enough. On the contrary, when you use <strong>few</strong>, it means not enough or too little.</br></br>  Example:</br> \t<li><i>A few employees have their presentations ready today.</i></br></br>   <strong>Note: few</strong> can have a positive meaning when it is followed by a noun that we don't want.</br></br>   <i>Example:</i> \t<li><i>I have few problems understanding the concept of exception.</i> "}, {"type": "example", "content": "At the end of this session, you will be able to manage a few keywords necessary to implement exception handling in your code."}, {"type": "example", "content": "You can have one, a few, or many \"catches\" depending on the number of operations you have in each class. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "Send a text to the end user with a few steps to check and recommend a possible solution."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "face"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to confront"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Exception handling is the method to face any error that can appear during the execution of a program or application. "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob11aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "foresee"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to anticipate; to come up with a hypothetical future event"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: When you write code, you have to foresee any possible error during its execution process because when any occurs, the end user will need to know what is going wrong."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob11aud02"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "enclose"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "to surround"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(verb)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: You have to enclose functions within this class having in mind that any error could occur with the operations inside it."}, {"type": "audio", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob11aud03"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "I/O"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "input and output"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(noun)"}