, "selected": 0, "correct": 1}], "id": 0, "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "pdf", "content": "enpe7n5le03ob11re7pdf01.pdf"}], "type": "question"}]}], "template_override": 1, "type": "DWS"}], "stimuli": [{"type": "welcome", "id": 0, "contents": [{}], "_data": [], "template_override": 1}, {"type": "audio", "_data": [], "title": "", "template_override": 1, "id": 1, "contents": [{"type": "slideshow", "value": {"images": [{"id": 0, "image": "enpe7n5le03ob11re2img01.jpg", "time": "100", "text": "Hello, everyone. In today's session, we will talk about the concept of exception handling in C# and some related topics. At the end of this session, you will be able to manage a few keywords necessary to implement exception handling in your code. Exception handling is the method to face any error that can appear during the execution of a program or application. When you write code, you have to foresee any possible error during its execution process because when any occurs, the end user will need to know what is going wrong. To do this, the first keyword you have is \"try.\" You have to enclose functions within this class having in mind that any error could occur with the operations inside it; someone could delete a file, change its name, etc."}, {"id": 1, "image": "enpe7n5le03ob11re2img02.jpg", "time": "56000", "text": "Then, you have the keyword \"catch.\" You can have one, a few, or many \"catches\" depending on the number of operations you have in each class. With this keyword, you will be able to set the reaction of the program or application each time an error appears; it could, for example, send a text to the end user with a few steps to check and recommend a possible solution. The third keyword we have is \"finally.\" With this keyword, you define that whether an error appears or not, the operations run up to the result needed. In the end, you can use the keyword \"throw.\" With this keyword, you will be able to send a new action through the code to verify no error from a chain of operations is left with no solution."}, {"id": 2, "image": "enpe7n5le03ob11re2img03.jpg", "time": "110000", "text": "To finish our session, I'd like you to remember a few exception classes: System.IO.IOException, System.DivideByZeroException, and System.OutOfMemoryException. The first one handles errors connected to I/O properties; the second one, the errors coming from dividing with zero, and the third one, handles errors resulting from insufficient free memory."}], "coverimg": "enpe7n5le03ob11re2img01.jpg", "audiosrc": "enpe7n5le03ob11re2aud01"}}]}], "_data": [{"contents": [{"type": "has_nav", "content": true}, {"type": "has_exercise_controls", "content": true}], "type": "lab_options"}]}]}